    "evidence_text": "BACKGROUND The relation between sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs) and body weight remains controversial.   \n\n\nOBJECTIVES We estimated the relationship between soft drink consumption and obesity and diabetes worldwide.   \n\n\nThese findings remained robust in low- and middle-income countries.   \n\n\nThe prevention of type 2 diabetes\n\nSugar-sweetened beverages, weight gain, and incidence of type 2 diabetes in young and middle-aged women.\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Weight gain and incidence of type 2 diabetes.   \n\n\nAverting Obesity and Type 2 Diabetes in India through Sugar-Sweetened Beverage Taxation: An Economic-Epidemiologic Modeling Study\n\nBACKGROUND Taxing sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs) has been proposed in high-income countries to reduce obesity and type 2 diabetes.\n\nAdded sugars were assessed by 3 24-h dietary recalls at baseline.\n\nNo associations were observed with consumption of added sugars from solid sources.\n\nNo patients were using statins, and use of ACE inhibitors, which also reduce diabetes incidence, was relatively low.\n\nType 2 diabetes and subsequent incidence of breast cancer in the Nurses' Health Study.\n\nOBJECTIVE Hyperinsulinemia may promote mammary carcinogenesis.",
